        },
        "description": "",
        "id": 4,
        "links": [],
        "title": "How to read this",
        "vizConfig": {
          "group": "text",
          "kind": "VizConfig",
          "spec": {
            "fieldConfig": {
              "defaults": {},
              "overrides": []
            },
            "options": {
              "mode": "markdown",
              "content": "### How to read this dashboard\n\n**Read the excess, not the hit rate.** Hit rate = CORRECT \u00f7 (CORRECT + INCORRECT), *neutral calls excluded*. But that number has no meaning on its own: under this grading scheme **randomly shuffled, skill-free calls score ~66%** at 30d, because HOLD is CORRECT when the price stays flat and WATCH is CORRECT when it moves, and those two carry **89% of all calls** \u2014 so almost any outcome lands on the good side of something. The headline tile is therefore **hit rate minus that null, in percentage points**: 0 means chance, positive means the calls beat it. The grey tile keeps the raw rate for continuity (measured 2026-08-19, null pinned by `scripts/check_null_baseline.py`).\n\nOne caveat the tiles cannot show: the +2.7pp excess measured in session 31 comes from *which instruments* get which action, not from timing. Shuffle the actions within each ticker \u2014 holding instrument choice fixed \u2014 and the excess goes to **\u22122.3pp**. Treat a small positive excess as \"not yet distinguishable from chance\", not as evidence.\n\n- \ud83d\udfe2 **CORRECT** \u2014 the call's thesis played out within the horizon.\n- \u26aa **NEUTRAL** \u2014 the move was too small to judge (not counted in the hit rate).\n- \ud83d\udd34 **INCORRECT** \u2014 the price moved against the call.\n\n**Decisiveness** = share of graded calls that were *not* neutral. Per-action hit rates have their *own* very different nulls (WATCH ~79%, HOLD ~40% at 30d), so they cannot be compared with each other or with the headline. All tiles and tables use the **30-day** horizon (long-term orientation); 7d appears on the trend chart as a short-term timing diagnostic. 90d, 6m (180d) and 1y series fill in as outcomes mature (first 90d \u2248 Aug 2026, first 6m \u2248 Nov 2026, first 1y \u2248 May 2027). Grading bands widen with the horizon (e.g. a 1-year BUY must beat +15%, not +4%) and scale to **each instrument's own measured volatility** (a tracker whose month moves 2.4% is graded on bands ~0.13\u00d7 a typical stock's, a small cap that moves 35% on ~1.9\u00d7 \u2014 so a verdict measures the call, not the beta). Asset class alone was too coarse: ETFs spanned 8.8\u00d7 inside it, and the extremes were graded on bands that decided the verdict before the call was read \u2014 the index scored 0 correct watches out of 22, ever, while the calmest funds collected free holds (corrected 2026-08-17; asset-class scaling 2026-07-30). In the sector table, **ETF** groups all exchange-traded funds (they have no sector of their own). Use the time picker (top-right) to scope a period.",
              "code": {
                "language": "plaintext",
                "showLineNumbers": false,
                "showMiniMap": false
              }
            }
          },
          "version": "13.2.0-28852654685"
        }
      }
    },
    "panel-5": {
      "kind": "Panel",
      "spec": {
        "data": {
          "kind": "QueryGroup",
          "spec": {
            "queries": [
              {
                "kind": "PanelQuery",
                "spec": {
                  "hidden": false,
                  "query": {
                    "datasource": {
                      "name": "cfadv004ogglcf"
                    },
                    "group": "mysql",
                    "kind": "DataQuery",
                    "spec": {
                      "editorMode": "code",
                      "format": "table",
                      "rawQuery": true,
                      "rawSql": "SELECT UNIX_TIMESTAMP(DATE(generated_at) - INTERVAL WEEKDAY(generated_at) DAY) AS time_sec, ROUND(100*SUM(verdict='CORRECT' AND horizon_days=30)/ NULLIF(SUM(verdict IN('CORRECT','INCORRECT') AND horizon_days=30),0),0) AS `30d hit %`, ROUND(100*SUM(verdict='CORRECT' AND horizon_days=7)/ NULLIF(SUM(verdict IN('CORRECT','INCORRECT') AND horizon_days=7),0),0) AS `7d hit %`, ROUND(100*SUM(verdict='CORRECT' AND horizon_days=90)/ NULLIF(SUM(verdict IN('CORRECT','INCORRECT') AND horizon_days=90),0),0) AS `90d hit %`, ROUND(100*SUM(verdict='CORRECT' AND horizon_days=180)/ NULLIF(SUM(verdict IN('CORRECT','INCORRECT') AND horizon_days=180),0),0) AS `180d hit %`, ROUND(100*SUM(verdict='CORRECT' AND horizon_days=365)/ NULLIF(SUM(verdict IN('CORRECT','INCORRECT') AND horizon_days=365),0),0) AS `365d hit %`, SUM(horizon_days=30 AND verdict IN('CORRECT','INCORRECT')) AS `30d decided`, 65.8 AS `null (chance)` FROM recommendation_outcomes WHERE $__timeFilter(generated_at) GROUP BY time_sec ORDER BY time_sec"
                    },
                    "version": "v0"
                  },
                  "refId": "A"
                }
              }
            ],
            "queryOptions": {},
            "transformations": []
          }
        },
        "description": "Weekly hit rate by horizon (Monday-anchored weeks). 30d (green) is the headline; 7d is a short-term timing diagnostic; 90d (blue), 6m (orange) and 1y (purple) fill in as they mature. The light bars (right axis) show how many 30-day calls were decided that week \u2014 thin bars mean a noisy hit rate. The dashed red line is the **null**: what shuffled, skill-free calls score under this grading scheme (65.8% at 30d and 7d; the 90d null is higher, ~76%). A series sitting on that line is measuring the scheme, not the system.",
        "id": 5,
        "links": [],
        "title": "Hit rate over time (weekly)",
